        '### 3. Central Sensitisation',
        'Central sensitisation (CS) is a state of amplified excitability in the central nervous system where symptom-processing circuits are sensitised — their activation threshold is lowered and their response magnitude is increased — by prior nociceptive and inflammatory input (Woolf, 2011). It is well-established in chronic musculoskeletal pain and is increasingly recognised as a contributor to PPCS.',
        '   - MECHANISM: The sustained NMDA receptor activation and glutamate excitotoxicity of the acute neurometabolic cascade, compounded by ongoing neuroinflammation, produce upregulation of NMDA receptor expression and increased synaptic weighting in pain-processing circuits. The result is a sensitised CNS that amplifies inputs which would normally be sub-threshold.',
        '   - CLINICAL EXPRESSION IN PPCS: Photophobia (light sensitivity), phonophobia (noise sensitivity), vestibular hypersensitivity (motion provokes dizziness at intensities that would not affect a healthy person), headache from minor cognitive or physical loads, and skin allodynia are all consistent with a sensitised symptom-processing system. The patient is not exaggerating — their CNS is genuinely amplifying those signals.',
